WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:04.033
A relationship is more than an invitation sent once.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.900
In our continuing planning example, the team is considering

3
00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:10.567
how people will hear about an opportunity and how

4
00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:14.700
they can respond. Before contacting anyone, be clear about

5
00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:17.700
the purpose, what the team can still change, who

6
00:00:17.722 --> 00:00:20.678
makes the decision, and when a response is useful.

7
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.122
These details help people decide whether and how they

8
00:00:24.144 --> 00:00:24.967
want to participate.

9
00:00:25.742 --> 00:00:28.364
It is also important to say what you do

10
00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:31.453
not yet know. If a decision has already been

11
00:00:31.476 --> 00:00:34.787
made, do not invite input as though every option

12
00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:37.764
remains open. If the team can change the wording

13
00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:41.676
of an invitation but not the statutory deadline, explain

14
00:00:42.076 --> 00:00:45.476
that difference. When you are unsure what can be

15
00:00:45.498 --> 00:00:49.031
changed, check with the responsible person before making a

16
00:00:49.053 --> 00:00:49.520
promise.

17
00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:54.470
Participation in this learning is individual and voluntary. The

18
00:00:54.492 --> 00:00:58.714
workplace action you consider may involve responsibilities, partners or

19
00:00:58.737 --> 00:01:01.626
review beyond your role. Use the learning to prepare

20
00:01:01.648 --> 00:01:04.670
a thoughtful question and the appropriate next step, not

21
00:01:04.692 --> 00:01:06.470
to assume authority you do not have.

22
00:01:07.503 --> 00:01:10.459
Ask how people would like to receive information and

23
00:01:10.481 --> 00:01:13.414
what would make it possible to respond. Depending on

24
00:01:13.437 --> 00:01:17.370
the work, that may involve language assistance, accessible materials,

25
00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.881
a workable time, another way to contribute, or support

26
00:01:20.903 --> 00:01:24.370
for participating. Check what current guidance permits and what

27
00:01:24.392 --> 00:01:27.659
arrangements are available. Do not assume that everyone reached

28
00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:30.170
through the same organization shares one preference.

29
00:01:30.791 --> 00:01:34.991
A community organization may offer valuable knowledge and relationships.

30
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.058
Ask what role it wants and can take, and

31
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.547
recognize that one partner cannot speak for everyone. Where

32
00:01:41.569 --> 00:01:45.058
appropriate and under the applicable process, plan for compensation,

33
00:01:45.347 --> 00:01:49.347
accessible participation and the time people need. Explain how

34
00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:51.591
their input will be considered and how the team

35
00:01:51.613 --> 00:01:52.969
expects to report back.

36
00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:57.158
If you cannot make a commitment yourself, identify who

37
00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.580
can answer before presenting it as settled.

38
00:02:00.261 --> 00:02:04.728
Protect information appropriately. Explain the purpose of a request,

39
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.217
how information will be used and any applicable limits,

40
00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:11.861
based on current procedures. For this lesson, your own

41
00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:14.417
notes can remain general. You do not need to

42
00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:18.906
record anyone's name, case information, health details, immigration status

43
00:02:19.106 --> 00:02:20.394
or other private information.

44
00:02:21.561 --> 00:02:25.494
There is an important distinction between an individual's choices,

45
00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:29.828
community engagement and government-to-government consultation. A person can tell

46
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.117
you what support or communication works for them. Community

47
00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:36.139
engagement can help a team understand a range of

48
00:02:36.161 --> 00:02:40.294
experiences and shape an open decision. Neither automatically fulfills

49
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:41.472
a government-to-government obligation.

50
00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:45.794
The existing One DSD direction is to defer to

51
00:02:45.817 --> 00:02:49.306
the Office of Indian Affairs and the DHS offices

52
00:02:49.328 --> 00:02:53.817
responsible for Tribal relations and consultation for substantive matters

53
00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:58.083
concerning Native American individuals, communities or Tribal Nations. If

54
00:02:58.106 --> 00:03:01.639
a proposed action may have Tribal implications, contact the

55
00:03:01.661 --> 00:03:05.617
appropriate designated office or liaison before planning engagement or

56
00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:09.372
consultation, and follow its guidance and the applicable process.

57
00:03:10.117 --> 00:03:14.294
Each Nation is a distinct sovereign government; a general

58
00:03:14.317 --> 00:03:16.517
community brief is not Nation-specific guidance.

59
00:03:17.283 --> 00:03:20.083
You do not need to decide here exactly which

60
00:03:20.106 --> 00:03:25.039
consultation process applies. Recognize the potential implication, identify the

61
00:03:25.061 --> 00:03:28.239
responsible office, and ask how to proceed. Do not

62
00:03:28.261 --> 00:03:31.128
present a general listening session, an invitation to a

63
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.883
community organization, or a brief you have read as

64
00:03:33.906 --> 00:03:35.883
a substitute for the designated process.

65
00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:41.094
Suppose the planning opportunity may affect a Tribal Nation.

66
00:03:41.561 --> 00:03:44.872
A team is considering a general listening session and

67
00:03:44.894 --> 00:03:48.761
is unsure whether another process is needed. Which step

68
00:03:48.783 --> 00:03:51.783
would you take? Choose one of the three responses

69
00:03:51.806 --> 00:03:55.494
on the page, then explore the feedback. Pause if

70
00:03:55.517 --> 00:03:57.272
you would like to think about what you can

71
00:03:57.294 --> 00:04:00.139
decide and who is responsible for the review.

72
00:04:02.072 --> 00:04:04.961
Consider what each option would mean for the team’s

73
00:04:04.983 --> 00:04:08.917
next step and the responsible offices. The feedback becomes

74
00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:10.450
available after you choose.

75
00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.849
Return to your work question. Write a general plan

76
00:04:14.871 --> 00:04:18.649
with five items: the purpose; what people may influence;

77
00:04:18.916 --> 00:04:22.693
the responsible decision maker; the support needed for participation;

78
00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.427
and how the team will respond after hearing from

79
00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:28.160
people. Add any question you need to take to

80
00:04:28.182 --> 00:04:31.049
a designated office or other responsible partner.

81
00:04:31.717 --> 00:04:34.761
If you do not know whether compensation, an interpreter,

82
00:04:35.072 --> 00:04:39.072
a particular format, or another arrangement is available, mark

83
00:04:39.094 --> 00:04:42.072
it as something to confirm. Do not put someone's

84
00:04:42.094 --> 00:04:45.361
private circumstances in your learning note. The value of

85
00:04:45.383 --> 00:04:48.250
your plan is that it makes responsibilities and unanswered

86
00:04:48.272 --> 00:04:51.094
questions visible before an invitation goes out.

87
00:04:51.594 --> 00:04:55.861
A relationship also needs follow-through. After a decision, tell

88
00:04:56.017 --> 00:04:59.461
participants what was considered, what changed, what did not

89
00:04:59.483 --> 00:05:02.883
change and why, as appropriate to the process. If

90
00:05:02.906 --> 00:05:05.572
some information cannot be shared, explain what you can

91
00:05:05.594 --> 00:05:08.617
say and where questions can go. People should not

92
00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:11.372
have to guess whether their contribution reached anyone.

93
00:05:12.504 --> 00:05:16.616
The community engagement and connections resources offer planning questions

94
00:05:16.638 --> 00:05:21.082
about purpose, influence and reporting back. The Minnesota Communities

95
00:05:21.104 --> 00:05:24.793
collection provides background for preparing questions, while the CLAS

96
00:05:24.816 --> 00:05:27.860
learning and the Equity Analysis Toolkit offer ways to

97
00:05:27.882 --> 00:05:31.371
examine access and a decision together. Use the standing

98
00:05:31.393 --> 00:05:35.149
Tribal deference and current designated guidance wherever they apply.

99
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.782
In the final part, we will bring the work

100
00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:42.804
together. You will prepare a small action and review

101
00:05:42.827 --> 00:05:46.093
plan, then consider how to tell whether a change

102
00:05:46.116 --> 00:05:48.871
helped the people it was intended to reach.
